sec.p:
Generative approaches relate transformations to hierarchical syntactic representations and feature configurations ([
10
]). Distributed Morphology separates syntactic structure from post-syntactic realization through mechanisms such as Vocabulary Insertion ([
26
]; [
21
]), whereas Word-and-Paradigm approaches emphasize relations among forms and morphosyntactic values within paradigms ([
36
]). Although these frameworks are not empirically interchangeable, they may assign different descriptive statuses to structurally related configurations. This creates a problem of classificatory instability: a comparable formal relation may be described through syntactic feature structure, post-syntactic realization, contextual allomorphy, phonological conditioning, or paradigmatic organization. The variation does not make the data irrelevant or reduce theoretical disagreement to a methodological difference; it shows that different explanatory mechanisms do not automatically provide a shared descriptive vocabulary.

The symbol Δ is adopted for its established association with change or difference in formal notation ([
1
]). Here, it marks a formally identifiable relation of change between linguistic configurations; it does not represent grammatical transformation as a numerical difference between values. The proposal treats transformation as a decomposable relation among an initial configuration, a structural intervention, and a resulting configuration. This draws a limited analogy with formal equations, which make explicit relations between terms and the operations connecting them. The analogy does not treat grammatical transformations as calculations or linguistic forms as numerical values; it motivates representing a process through components and conditions that can be identified independently.

This study adopts a comparative formal perspective that distinguishes structural classification from framework-specific explanation ([
33
]; [
28
]); the dataset, selection principles, and analytical protocol are specified in Section 5, while the formal constraints and conditions of revision are set out in Section 6.

Minimalism relates grammatical alternations to syntactic feature structure and operations over hierarchical representations ([
8
]). Distributed Morphology separates syntactic feature structure from post-syntactic realization through Vocabulary Insertion and operations such as Fusion or Impoverishment ([
26
]; [
23
]; [
22
]). Word-and-Paradigm morphology treats inflection through relations among forms and morphosyntactic values within paradigms ([
36
]; [
12
]; [
2
]). Construction Grammar analyzes related patterns through form-meaning pairings, inheritance, and analogical generalization rather than through transformations over underlying representations ([
24
]; [
13
]; [
5
]).

For example, an internal alternation within a paradigm may be described through syntactic feature configurations, post-syntactic realization or Vocabulary Insertion, phonological adjustment, or paradigmatic relations among forms. Distributed Morphology distinguishes syntactic structure from morphological realization and provides mechanisms for post-syntactic operations and Vocabulary Insertion ([
26
]; [
23
]; [
22
]). Word-and-Paradigm approaches instead treat relations among forms and feature values within a paradigm as central to morphological analysis ([
30
]; [
36
]). These descriptions need not be mutually incompatible, but they do not provide a shared vocabulary for classifying the structural relation itself.

The proposal also draws on the notion of a comparative concept ([
28
]), extending its comparative logic to the analysis of relations across theoretical frameworks rather than deriving its classificatory categories from any single framework. Its usefulness depends on whether its activation criteria can be applied consistently to independently described data. The following case study examines the English plural as a limited illustration of how framework-specific analyses and the proposed Δ description may be compared without treating their differences as already resolved.

The regular English plural provides a focused case for examining morphological realization within Distributed Morphology ([
26
]; [
21
]). The analysis is limited to the framework-specific account of plural realization. It does not apply the Δ architecture, nor does it assess the empirical adequacy of Distributed Morphology.

In Distributed Morphology, syntactic structure is distinguished from morphological and phonological realization ([
23
]). Syntax operates over abstract feature bundles. In a regular English plural noun, the plural feature may be represented on a functional Number head. At Vocabulary Insertion, the regular plural is realized by a single exponent, /z/ ([
21
]).

The Subset Principle concerns the insertion of Vocabulary Items whose feature specifications are compatible with a terminal node ([
26
]). It does not select [s], [z], and [ɪz] as three distinct regular-plural exponents in Embick’s analysis.

This distinction is relevant to the comparative discussion because analyses must not conflate morphological realization with phonological derivation. Distributed Morphology explains the regular plural through the relation between abstract morphosyntactic structure, Vocabulary Insertion, and phonological realization. Word-and-Paradigm morphology instead treats plural forms as realizations of morphosyntactic values within a paradigm ([
36
]; [
2
]). These frameworks differ in explanatory architecture, without requiring the present section to apply a separate classificatory model.

The position of c relative to R
0
is specified exclusively at the microstructural level: c [+ (R
0
)] for prefixal material, c [(+R
0
)] for infixal material, and c [(R
0
)+] for suffixal material. These positional specifications may be combined with the relevant component index, for example c1 [(R
0
)+] or c
1
[(R
0
)+].

Here, c1 [(R
0
)+] and c2 [(R
0
)+] specify suffixal components. This suffixal positional specification is illustrative; in other cases, the macrostructural components n1 and n2 may correspond to microstructural components c1 and c2 in prefixal or infixal positions. The two formulas describe the same intervention at different analytical levels: the first identifies the relational modification, while the second specifies its internal structural realization.

The subscript 0 does not indicate a null morpheme, zero exponent, empty position, or omitted structural element; it marks the abstract representation of the relational nucleus. Within C [(R
0
+)], + marks the informational activation of that nucleus. It does not function here as an operator of integration or addition, or specify the position of dependent material. Those functions are distinct: + is operatorial in Δ (+), while in c [+(R
0
)], c [(+R
0
)], and c [(R
0
)+] its placement specifies prefixal, infixal, and suffixal material, respectively.

The positional use of “+” applies only to expressions specifying dependent material relative to R
0
. The convention is uniform: c [+(R
0
)] = prefixal material; c [(+R
0
)] = infixal material; c [(R
0
)+] = suffixal material.

At the configurational level, correlated substitution is specified according to the position of the affected dependent material: C [ (R
0
)−/+] represents suffixal substitution; C [−/+ (R
0
)] represents prefixal substitution; and C [(−/+R
0
)] represents infixal substitution. These notations specify the position and substitutional status of the initial configuration; they do not introduce additional operators.

Accordingly, the relevant initial configuration is represented as 1C [(R
0
)−/+], 1C [−/+ (R
0
)], or

1C [(−/+R
0
)], while the stabilized result is represented as 2C [(R
0
+)]. The correlated elimination and introduction of the specific dependent components are represented separately through expressions such as {−c1 [(R
0
)+] + c2 [(R
0
)+]}.

In a microstructural formula such as: 1C [ (R
0
)−/+]{−c1 [(R
0
)+] + c2 [(R
0
)+]} = 2C [ (R
0
+)], the sign “+” marks the inclusion of c2 within the specified substitutional configuration. It does not itself perform the substitution and does not create an additional operatorial value. The substitutional regime is identified through the complete configuration containing the correlated elimination of c1 and introduction of c2.

The Romanian relation zi → zile illustrates the directional correspondence between singular and plural forms of the same lexical unit ([
20
]). Δ (→) represents their relation without determining how the plural material is realized. If that material contribution is analyzed separately, it may be classified as an additive intervention, provided that no pre-existing material is eliminated or replaced in the same structural position.

The relation casă → case illustrates this distinction ([
16
]). It can be represented directionally, but Δ (→) alone does not classify its internal modification: because -ă is eliminated and -e introduced in the same identified position, the executional intervention is classified as Δ (−/+).

The Romanian relation merg → mergeam ([
18
]) illustrates Δ (+): inflectional material is integrated into the verbal configuration, with no pre-existing material eliminated, replaced, or repositioned in the same identified structural position. By contrast, casă → case ([
16
]) is not additive under this analysis. The final -ă is eliminated and replaced by -e in the same position, so the intervention is classified as Δ (−/+).

At the microstructural level, the corresponding relation is represented as c [+ (R
0
)] > C1 [+ (R
0
)] = C2 [(R
0
+)]. The component c [+ (R
0
)] represents dependent prefixal material; C1 identifies the lexical configuration associated with the initial base, and C2 the resulting configuration. Here, > specifies the directional distribution of the dependent component relative to R
0
. C1 and C2 mark correlated microstructural configurations, not successive phases of one configuration, grammatical values, or distinct operators. Infixal and suffixal material are represented as c [(+R
0
)] and c [(R
0
)+], respectively.

Greek ο άντρας illustrates the analysis: ο >/< άντρας = ο άντρας represents the definite article ο as a dependent determiner of άντρας, which remains the lexical base. Their functional relation forms a determiner-noun configuration without modifying the internal material of either unit ([
6
]).

The regime includes two types of positional reorganization. In functional positional reorganization, the change in position produces an updated grammatical or semantic function, which must be explicitly identified; positional difference alone is insufficient. In non-functional positional reorganization, the components change position without any change in their grammatical or semantic function. When empirical evidence supports such an analysis, this second type may correspond to metathesis or structural permutation ([
3
]).

An attested example of non-functional positional reorganization is the English alternation ask ~ aks: /s/ and /k/ exchange their relative positions, while the material inventory and lexical meaning remain unchanged ([
34
]). Alongside metathesis and structural permutation, Δ (~) also covers cases where positional redistribution changes a component’s grammatical or semantic function without changing the material inventory.

The forms C [(R
0
)−/+], C [−/+ (R
0
)], and C [(−/+R
0
)] specify the substitutional status and position of dependent material in an initial microstructural configuration: suffixal, prefixal, and infixal, respectively. They do not denote additional operators or independent transformations. These positional distinctions are not represented at the macrostructural level.

Δ (±) covers morphological paradigms in which grammatical variation is distributed across correlated forms without changing lexical identity ([
36
]; [
2
]). Appendix B.4.8, “Visual Illustration of the Δ (±) Cumulative Paradigmatic Flexibility Regime,” illustrates the regime.

The construction of the model follows a structured procedure consisting of three stages: (i) identification of recurrent patterns of structural intervention, (ii) formalization of these patterns through macrostructural and microstructural representations, and (iii) specification of activation criteria that allow the differentiation of operatorial regimes. Each regime is defined through a distinct structural condition and is delimited from the others by the exclusion of overlapping criteria at the same representational level. The design is oriented toward formal consistency and internal differentiation. This deductive and model-based orientation is consistent with established approaches to theoretical linguistic analysis ([
33
]).

This approach allows the analysis to remain focused on the identification of structural regularities while keeping the limits of the dataset explicit. It follows the use of comparative concepts in cross- linguistic research, where comparability depends on clearly stated criteria rather than on an assumption that the selected examples constitute a typological sample ([
28
]).

To make the exclusivity of the operatorial regimes explicit, this section applies the analysis protocol step by step to a borderline case in which multiple classifications may initially appear possible: the alternation casă (“house”) → case (“houses”) ([
16
]).

At the microstructural level, this relation is expressed by the formula: 1C [(R
0
)-/+]{−c1 [(R
0
)+] + c2 [(R
0
)+]} = 2C [(R
0
+)], where 1C [(R
0
)−/+] corresponds to the initial structure (“casă”), −c1 [(R
0
)+] represents the elimination of the final segment “-ă”, +c2 [(R
0
)+] represents the introduction of the segment “-e” in the same structural position, and 2C [(R
0
+)] designates the resulting structure (“case”). This formula expresses a simultaneous substitution carried out in a single operatorial act, corresponding to the Δ (−/+) regime, illustrated in Appendix B, Figure 10, “Visual Illustration of the Δ (−/+) Substitutional Regime.”

Applying the protocol to go → went ([
35
]) yields a boundary result:

The analysis first identifies the representational level at which the claim is made. A relational claim concerns the correspondence or functional relation between configurations, whereas an executional claim concerns the internal modification of dependent material. Relational and executional descriptions may both apply to the same phenomenon only when they are stated in separate representations.The analysis then identifies the structural type of intervention. Oriented correspondence activates Δ (→); integration of dependent material activates Δ (+); an explicitly identified semantic or grammatical reorientation associated with dependent material activates Δ (><); correlated elimination and replacement in the same position activate Δ (−/+); relational coupling of already constituted units activates Δ (>/<); elimination activates Δ (−); positional reorganization of unchanged material activates Δ (~); and cumulative variation across at least three correlated realizations activates Δ (±).Where a configuration presents more than one relevant property, the analysis determines whether those properties are claimed at the same representational level or at distinct levels. A single representational level receives only one regime assignment. Complementary descriptions remain possible only when they concern different levels. Thus, in cat → cats ([
31
]), Δ (+) classifies the executional integration of plural material. In trust → distrust ([
32
]), Δ (+) classifies the executional integration of the prefix dis-, whereas Δ (><) classifies the semantic-relational reorientation produced by that prefix. These are not competing assignments because they concern distinct representational levels. By contrast, the Greek configuration ο >/< άντρας = ο άντρας ([
6
]) is classified through Δ (>/<), because it concerns the functional relation between already constituted units and does not claim the internal integration of material.The proposed classification is finally tested through exclusion. A regime is retained only when its activation condition is satisfied and the defining conditions of competing regimes at the same representational level are not satisfied. Where no common R
0
can be retained, the relation is treated as the beginning of a new operatorial cycle rather than as an internal executional transformation. These decision rules are summarized in Appendix B, Figure 3, “Decision Rules for Regime Assignment.”

For the present analysis, the relevant fact is the integration of plural material into the nominal configuration, classified through Δ (+). This classification concerns the structural intervention at the selected representational level. It does not classify the phonological realization of the plural exponent as [s], [z], or [ɪz], or treat their alternation as a series of additive, substitutive, or positional interventions ([
21
]).

Within a Minimalist perspective, the contrast may be related to a difference in syntactic feature structure and to the morphological realization of those features. This is a comparative characterization rather than a claim that Minimalist literature provides a specific published derivation of urs → ursoaică through Merge or another particular syntactic operation ([
10
]).

Within Distributed Morphology, the relation may be described through the distinction between abstract morphosyntactic features and their post-syntactic realization by Vocabulary Insertion. Any treatment of the sequence -oaică as an inserted exponent or as a complex realization must likewise be understood as a comparative reconstruction, not as an attributed published analysis of this Romanian form ([
26
]; [
21
]).

Within Word-and-Paradigm morphology, the relation may be described as a relation between lexemes and morphosyntactic or lexical values within a paradigm. This perspective does not require the analysis to derive the resulting form through the same type of internally ordered operation assumed by component-based approaches ([
36
]; [
2
]).
